The diff under review is the HEAD commit (#399, docs-only). The branch is `main` at `origin/main` with a clean working tree, so the range and working-tree diffs were empty and I fell back to `git diff HEAD~1`. I'm printing the findings instead of posting them, because there is no PR to comment on. I applied no fixes: each finding turns on a scheduling or exit-criterion decision, and `docs/roadmap.md` is the project owner's call.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 87,
    "summary": "The new P3 exit criterion requires CIs under both blind and sighted recipes, but RFC-0007 (and the added E4 note) make a portfolio of the winning recipes a second GEPA pass that runs after P3.",
    "failure_scenario": "GEPA v1 in P3 evolves one proposer prompt. The exit demands bootstrap CIs under both the blind and the sighted recipe, which means either running GEPA twice or fixing a recipe before E2 has chosen winners. An implementer reading P3 cannot tell which recipe GEPA v1 optimizes, or whether the second recipe is evaluated only, so the exit can be judged met or unmet depending on the reading."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 90,
    "summary": "The criterion 'bug-pair discrimination on every usable pair' is vacuously true on the corpus P3 uses.",
    "failure_scenario": "RFC-0007 says Tier 0 has no usable bug pair today and that M2 (bug-pair discrimination) starts at E1. P3 runs on Tier 0 plus mutants, so the quantifier ranges over an empty set and the criterion passes with zero evidence. Only the 'not \"the proposer is good\"' framing stops this from being a hollow gate. The exit should require a minimum pair count or say explicitly that M2 is out of P3."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 95,
    "summary": "P4 and P5 are now both labelled 'Dec–Jan' with P4 due 2027-01-15 and P5 due 2027-01-31, while P4's stated scope grew and its exit criterion did not.",
    "failure_scenario": "P3 is due 2026-12-18, so P4 gets about four weeks and overlaps P5. P5's exit needs a 'clean live demo on a non-toy example', which depends on P4's real-code result. RFC-0007 (E2–E4, the kernel pilot gated on RFC-0008, and a second GEPA pass) puts that work in P4. The P4 exit here still reads 'loop completes on ≥1 real-world C module'. A slip of one or two weeks leaves P5 with no demo target. The roadmap neither flags this dependency nor says which P4 items are cut first, even though ADR-0006 says P4's scope absorbs the slip."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 58,
    "summary": "The re-dating note gives an unclear reason and records milestone due dates in prose that can drift from the GitHub milestones.",
    "failure_scenario": "'W3 and W4 had no sub-issues yet' does not explain why P2 and P3 overran. The four due dates are hard-coded in the roadmap while CLAUDE.md says live tracking is on GitHub milestones. The next re-date can change the milestones and leave this note stale, so the roadmap contradicts the tracker. The note should point to the milestones or say the dates are a snapshot."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/design/0007-what-should-we-prove-proposer-at-project-scale.md",
    "line": 239,
    "summary": "The E4 note cites ADR-0006 for the claim that 'E2–E4 are P4's real-code work', but ADR-0006 says nothing about E-numbered experiments.",
    "failure_scenario": "ADR-0006 only sequences P2–P3 (property-gen, grading, GEPA) before P4 (real code). The mapping of E2/E3/E4 onto P4 comes from this RFC, not the ADR. A reader checking the cited ADR finds no support, and the RFC reads as if the ADR had ratified its experiment plan. E2 (Tier 1 replay) is also not obviously 'real code', since the same RFC defines Tier 1 separately from the kernel pilot, which is E3."
  }
]
```
